\begin{proof}
Let
\[
S=\sum_{k=1}^{p-1}\frac1k=\frac ab.
\]
The common denominator \((p-1)!\) is not divisible by \(p\), so write
\[
S=\frac{N}{(p-1)!}
\]
for some integer \(N\). In modulo \(p\), pair each index \(k\) with \(p-k\). Because \(p\) is odd, these are distinct and exhaust \(1,\ldots,p-1\). Moreover,
\[
\frac1k+\frac1{p-k}
\equiv \frac1k+\frac1{-k}
\equiv0\pmod p.
\]
Therefore \(N\equiv0\pmod p\), and hence \(S\equiv0\pmod p\). If \(S=A/B\) is in lowest terms, then \(B\mid(p-1)!\), so \(p\nmid B\). The congruence therefore implies \(p\mid A\). Since \(a/b=A/B\) and both fractions represent the same rational number, there is an integer \(t\) such that \(a=At\). Thus \(p\mid a\).
\end{proof}
